## Why This Problem Is Asked

This problem explores non-overlapping palindromic substring selection using a two-dimensional DP that tracks prefix length and the number of selected substrings. It tests palindrome precomputation, transition logic, and solution reconstruction under constraints.

## Key Concepts

- **Palindrome table**: pal[l][r] indicates whether s[l..r] is a palindrome
- **DP state**: dp[i][t] represents the maximum total length obtainable from prefix s[0..i] using exactly t substrings
- **Transition**: Either skip the current position or end a palindrome at i (starting at some l)
- **Reconstruction**: Use parent pointers to backtrack and retrieve the selected substrings

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPalindromes(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const pal = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(false));

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pal[i][i] = true;
  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let l = 0; l + len - 1 < n; l++) {
      const r = l + len - 1;
      if (s[l] === s[r] && (len === 2 || pal[l+1][r-1])) {
        pal[l][r] = true;
      }
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const parent = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k+1).fill(null));

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1]?.[t] ?? 0;
      parent[i][t] = { skip: true };

      for (let l = 0; l <= i; l++) {
        if (pal[l][i]) {
          const prev = l > 0 ? dp[l-1][t-1] : 0;
          const val = prev + (i - l + 1);
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            parent[i][t] = { skip: false, l, t: t-1 };
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const result = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (i >= 0 && t > 0) {
    const choice = parent[i][t];
    if (!choice.skip) {
      result.unshift([choice.l, i]);
      t = choice.t;
      i = choice.l - 1;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }

  return { maxLength: dp[n-1][k], substrings: result };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a three-dimensional DP that models constrained path planning with a binary obstacle grid and a budget of unblocks. Requires careful state definition, transitions, and path reconstruction. 

## Key Concepts
- DP with an extra dimension for unblocks (t)
- Handling blocked vs unblocked cells in transitions
- Path reconstruction from 3D DP table
- Time/space trade-offs and rolling optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPath(grid, blocked, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: m}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG)));
  if (!blocked[0][0]) dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  else if (k > 0) dp[0][0][1] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        const val = grid[i][j];
        if (i > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i-1][j][t] + val * (blocked[i][j] ? 0 : 1));
          if (blocked[i][j] && t > 0) dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i-1][j][t-1] + val);
        }
        if (j > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t] + val * (blocked[i][j] ? 0 : 1));
          if (blocked[i][j] && t > 0) dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t-1] + val);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n-1][m-1][0];
}
